
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To configure the network on a Linux machine is an important skill.<br>You have already seen <a href="https://tomsitcafe.com/2026/11/06/systemd-configure-your-network-with-networkd/">how to do it with systemd-networkd</a>.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">NetworkManager gives you another way to do it: nmcli.</p>



<span id="more-9613"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In the previous example you created a network bridge interface.<br>Then you added a physical card to the bridge.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The chore is the same. The tool is different.<br></p>



<h3 id="org42d5443" class="wp-block-heading">Create The Bridge Interface</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In newer versions of NetworkManager, the terminology has changed.<br>In Debian 13 these are the commands to create the bridge,<br>then to attach a physical device to it.<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code" id="org39620a3">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli device status</div><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli connection add type bridge con-name bridge0 ifname bridge0</div><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli connection add type ethernet slave-type bridge con-name &apos;Bridge connection 1&apos; ifname enp2s0 master bridge0</div><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli connection modify bridge0 connection.autoconnect-slaves 1</div><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli connection up bridge0</div><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli device status</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<h3 id="orgf67636b" class="wp-block-heading">Add A Static IP Address</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To add a static IP address to the newly created bridge device, run<br>the following commands.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Adjust the IP configuration to match your network.<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code" id="org71bb851">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli connection modify bridge0 ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.3/24</div><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli connection modify bridge0 ipv4.gateway 192.168.1.1</div><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli connection modify bridge0 ipv4.dns &quot;8.8.8.8 1.1.1.1&quot;</div><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli connection modify bridge0 ipv4.method manual</div><div class="cm-line">sudo nmcli connection up bridge0</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<h3 id="org32bdb90" class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For a sysadmin it is a very important skill to set up the networking.<br>There are different ways to do it.<br></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>systemd-networkd</li>



<li>ifupdown</li>



<li>NetworkManager and nmcli</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The chore is the same, but you use different tools to achieve the expected outcome.</p>
